* The Skillplane redirect-registration defect remains a consumer/provider regression case, not a reason to merge all application auth into McpFn.

## Key risks and mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
| -- | -- |
| Convenience API hides authorization decisions | Require explicit context, visibility, authorize, confirmation, and idempotency hooks |
| Client/testing/inspector implementations diverge | One headless session engine; surfaces remain adapters |
| AuthFn is mistaken for an MCP authorization server | Document boundary; separate adapter from authorization-server mode |
| OAuth secrets leak through diagnostics | Construct redacted events; strict fixtures; size caps; negative tests |
| Loopback/browser behavior varies by runtime | Explicit callback adapters and supported-runtime matrix |
| Provider-shaped profiles become stale | Version fixtures; record provenance; schedule live drift checks |
| Official SDK/conformance evolves | Pin versions; compatibility lanes; reviewed upgrades |
| Package split causes churn | Phase 0 ADR, compatibility re-exports, staged deprecation |
| Destructive calls replay after refresh/reconnect | No implicit replay without idempotency and policy approval |
